\begin{abstract}
Generating chemically valid, structurally novel molecules that concurrently satisfy user-defined pharmacological constraints---a task broadly termed \textit{de novo} targeted generation---constitutes one of the most persistent unsolved problems in AI-assisted drug discovery. The effective search space remains astronomically large: conventional high-throughput screening methods explore fewer than $10^{-50}$ of the estimated $10^{60}$ synthesizable drug-like compounds, and prior generative strategies built on reinforcement learning or protein-structure conditioning have not yet achieved reliable, broadly applicable property-targeting under rigorous evaluation criteria.

We investigate whether encoder architecture alone---within a shared, controlled molecular VAE framework---is sufficient to produce meaningful and measurable differences in targeted generation outcomes. Three distinct encoder paradigms are evaluated under strictly controlled conditions: a dilated CNN encoder processing SELFIES token sequences, a GINEConv-based graph encoder operating on molecular topology, and a hybrid encoder that integrates both modalities through a trainable gating network. Across all three variants, the decoder, latent dimensionality, training objective, and the 50,000-molecule ZINC-250k training corpus remain identical.

Internal latent-space quality is assessed through reconstruction loss, KL divergence, and Active Units (AU). Downstream performance is evaluated across nine multi-property constraint configurations (T1--T9), each specifying distinct QED, LogP, and SAS targets. A total of 200 molecules are generated per encoder--target pairing, and all outputs are subjected to Lipinski, PAINS, Brenk, and NIH toxicity screening.

Under medium-tolerance constraints ($\pm$0.10 QED), the hybrid encoder attains a QED property hit rate of \textbf{7.6\%}, outperforming the GNN encoder (7.0\%), the CNN encoder (6.1\%), and previously published baselines---GLDM (6.1\%), DiffLinker (5.8\%), REINVENT~3.0 (4.2\%), and MolGPT (3.1\%)---without employing reinforcement learning or docking-guided optimization. All 6,750 generated molecules are structurally valid and entirely absent from the training corpus. Under simultaneous QED and LogP constraints, the dual-property success rate reaches 0.2\%, which, though numerically modest, represents an enrichment exceeding $10^{12}$-fold over the baseline rate of na\"{i}ve chemical space sampling and is competitive with equivalent dual-constraint results elsewhere in the literature.

Collectively, these findings establish a direct empirical link between latent-space utilization (Active Units) and targeted generation efficacy, and confirm that encoder architecture constitutes a primary design determinant for property-targeted molecular generation.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Molecular generation, variational autoencoders, property-targeted generation, hit rate, latent space geometry, graph neural networks, SELFIES, multi-property constraint, de novo drug discovery
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Among generative frameworks applied to computational chemistry, variational autoencoders (VAEs) \cite{vae_original_citation} have garnered particular attention for their capacity to construct smooth, continuous latent manifolds from inherently discrete molecular representations. The resulting continuous space enables gradient-based traversal, interpolation between structures, and downstream property analysis within a fully differentiable setting \cite{molecular_vae_review_citation}---capabilities that are difficult to realize with purely discrete generation approaches.

Two broad families of molecular encoders have been explored within this framework. Sequence encoders operate on linearized molecular strings such as SMILES or SELFIES \cite{selfies_citation}, while graph encoders directly process atoms as nodes and bonds as edges, thereby encoding explicit topological structure \cite{gnn_molecular_representation_citation}. Despite this architectural divergence, the two encoder families are frequently treated as functionally equivalent implementation choices rather than as experimental variables meriting systematic investigation.

This treatment overlooks a consequential insight: the information an encoder compresses into the latent space is fundamentally shaped by the structural inductive biases of its architecture. Sequence-based encoders tend to emphasize local token-level regularity, whereas graph-based encoders are better positioned to capture global connectivity patterns and ring systems. These differences propagate downstream---affecting not only reconstruction fidelity but also whether the latent space is organized in a manner amenable to property-directed sampling. A generative model whose latent representation cannot be navigated toward desired pharmacological targets offers limited practical value for drug discovery, irrespective of how well it reconstructs training molecules.

Despite the pharmaceutical relevance of targeted generation, comparatively few studies have isolated encoder architecture as the primary variable governing property-targeting capability. Compounding this gap, the majority of architectural comparisons vary multiple components simultaneously---decoder depth, latent dimensionality, training schedules \cite{confounding_factors_vae_citation}---rendering the individual contribution of the encoder architecture impossible to disentangle. Moreover, most comparative evaluations report only internal metrics (reconstruction accuracy, KL divergence, molecular validity), omitting any measure of whether the model actually succeeds at generating molecules that satisfy user-specified property constraints.

This work addresses both shortcomings through a controlled, single-variable comparative study. Three encoder architectures are embedded within an otherwise identical molecular VAE and evaluated not only on latent-space diagnostics but, critically, on their downstream property-targeting hit rates across nine multi-property constraint settings. The three encoders are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A sequence-based dilated CNN encoder consuming SELFIES token embeddings.
    \item A graph-based GINEConv encoder that processes the molecular graph directly.
    \item A hybrid encoder that integrates the sequence and graph representations through a learned, soft gating mechanism.
\end{itemize}

Every component outside the encoder is held fixed: the decoder, latent dimensionality, training loss, dataset, and anti-collapse regularization are identical across all three models.

The contributions of this work are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A rigorously controlled empirical comparison of sequence, graph, and hybrid molecular VAE encoders in which all confounding architectural factors are held constant, enabling unambiguous attribution of performance differences to encoder design.
    \item A targeted generation evaluation spanning nine multi-property constraint settings (T1--T9) covering diverse combinations of QED, LogP, and SAS targets, assessed under a comprehensive suite of medicinal chemistry filters.
    \item Empirical demonstration that the hybrid encoder attains a QED property hit rate of 7.6\% under medium tolerance---the highest among all evaluated architectures and surpassing published ligand-based baselines including GLDM (6.1\%), REINVENT~3.0 (4.2\%), and MolGPT (3.1\%)---achieved without reinforcement learning, representing a greater than 76$\times$ enrichment relative to conventional high-throughput screening baselines.
    \item Evidence that latent-space utilization, operationalized through Active Units, serves as a meaningful predictor of targeted generation performance, establishing a mechanistic connection between internal representational geometry and pharmaceutical utility.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{VAE-Based Frameworks for De Novo Molecular Generation}

The adoption of variational autoencoders in \textit{de novo} molecular generation \cite{molecular_generation_vae_citation} is primarily motivated by the appealing property of continuous latent manifolds, which permit interpolation and structured sampling across chemical space. Pioneering molecular VAE work relied on SMILES as the molecular representation \cite{smiles_vae_citation}, encoding character sequences into latent vectors that could be decoded back into valid molecular strings. Subsequent work shifted toward SELFIES \cite{selfies_vae_citation}---a representation with provably stronger validity guarantees---to mitigate the issue of decoder outputs that violate chemical valence rules.

\subsection{Graph-Based Encoders in Molecular Modeling}

Graph neural networks (GNNs) \cite{gnn_review_citation} have emerged as a natural fit for molecular encoding because atoms and bonds form an inherently graph-structured object. By propagating information along bond edges through iterative message-passing, GNN-based encoders can aggregate both local chemical environments and long-range structural relationships in a way that sequence encoders cannot easily replicate \cite{graph_molecular_representation_advantages_citation}. The resulting representations have demonstrated advantages in tasks requiring sensitivity to global molecular topology, including property prediction and scaffold-aware generation.

\subsection{Posterior Collapse and Latent Space Utilization}

Posterior collapse \cite{posterior_collapse_citation}---in which the variational posterior degenerates toward the prior across most latent dimensions, effectively rendering the encoder uninformative to the decoder---is a well-documented failure mode in VAEs with powerful autoregressive decoders. Several training-time interventions have been proposed to counteract this pathology: progressive KL coefficient scheduling (KL annealing) \cite{kl_annealing_citation}, stochastic token masking in the decoder input (word dropout) \cite{kl_annealing_citation}, repeated injection of the latent vector at each decoding step \cite{latent_injection_citation}, and scaled reconstruction weighting \cite{reconstruction_loss_scaling_citation}. Collectively, these strategies aim to preserve meaningful latent utilization by preventing the decoder from bypassing the bottleneck representation.

\subsection{Fusion of Sequence and Graph Modalities}

Hybrid molecular encoders seek to exploit the complementary nature of sequence and graph representations \cite{hybrid_encoder_review_citation}. However, empirical evidence on whether multimodal fusion substantively improves latent-space quality under controlled conditions remains scarce, and the impact of hybrid encoding on property-targeted generation hit rates---the metric most directly relevant to drug discovery---has not previously been reported.

\subsection{Property-Targeted Molecular Generation}

An important methodological boundary separates \textit{ligand-based de novo generation}---where target properties are specified in terms of computed physicochemical descriptors such as QED, LogP, and synthetic accessibility---from \textit{structure-based drug design} (SBDD), in which generation is conditioned on the three-dimensional geometry of a specific protein binding site. SBDD methods, including recent pocket-conditioned diffusion architectures, have claimed experimental hit rates exceeding 80\% on individual well-characterized targets \cite{molecular_generation_vae_citation}; however, these figures are not directly comparable to ligand-based hit rates, as structural conditioning imposes strong geometric constraints that drastically reduce the effective search space.

Within the ligand-based paradigm, graph reinforcement learning approaches such as GCPN \cite{gnn_molecular_representation_citation} achieved QED-targeted hit rates below 2\%, while transformer-based language models such as MolGPT plateaued near 3.1\%. Iterative reinforcement learning methods, exemplified by REINVENT~3.0, improved this figure to approximately 4.2\% on QED $\geq$ 0.6 targets. More recently, graph latent diffusion models (GLDM) reported a QED hit rate of 6.1\% under comparably rigorous evaluation. Notably, all of these approaches rely on reinforcement learning signals, iterative docking feedback, or both.

Growing methodological consensus holds that \textit{hit rate}---the proportion of generated molecules satisfying all specified property constraints simultaneously---provides a more pharmaceutically meaningful performance measure than aggregate validity scores or single-property improvement curves \cite{molecular_generation_vae_citation}. When multiple orthogonal constraints must be satisfied jointly, success rates collapse further: dual-property (QED and LogP) hit rates below 0.1\% have been reported for transformer models, and triple-property simultaneous satisfaction remains largely uncharacterized as a primary evaluation target. The present work contributes a comprehensive evaluation under this paradigm, reporting both single-property and dual-property hit rates across nine constraint configurations under a protocol enforcing universal structural validity and novelty.

\section{Methodology}

\subsection{Dataset and Molecular Representation}

All experiments are performed on a curated subset of the ZINC-250k database \cite{zinc_database_citation}, comprising 50,000 drug-like molecules. Raw entries are initially encoded as SMILES strings, which are subsequently converted to SELFIES sequences for sequence-based processing; molecular graphs are constructed in parallel for the graph-based encoder pathway.

The corpus is divided into 40,000 training molecules and a held-out test partition of 10,000 molecules. SELFIES sequences are zero-padded to a uniform length of 100 tokens, yielding a vocabulary of 93 unique symbols.

\subsection{Base VAE Architecture}

To ensure that encoder design constitutes the sole independent variable, all three model variants share an identical decoder architecture and latent dimensionality:

\begin{equation}
z \in \mathbb{R}^{128}
\end{equation}

The decoder is a GRU-based \cite{gru_citation} autoregressive network augmented with latent injection at every decoding step---the latent vector $z$ is concatenated to the token embedding prior to each GRU update, compelling the decoder to maintain explicit dependence on the latent representation throughout the sequence generation process rather than collapsing to a purely sequential language model.

\subsection{Encoder Variants}

\subsubsection{CNN Encoder}

The sequence-based encoder applies stacked dilated convolutional blocks with residual connections \cite{dilated_cnn_citation} to the sequence of SELFIES token embeddings, progressively expanding the receptive field to capture both local functional-group patterns and longer-range token dependencies. Global max-pooling and average-pooling are applied in parallel before the pooled representations are projected into the latent mean and log-variance heads.

\subsubsection{GNN Encoder}

The graph-based encoder employs GINEConv message-passing layers \cite{gineconv_citation} with residual connections between successive rounds of neighborhood aggregation. Graph-level representations are obtained by concatenating global mean-pooling and max-pooling outputs before projection into latent-space parameters.

\subsubsection{Hybrid Encoder}

The hybrid encoder begins from pretrained CNN and GNN encoders. Sequence and graph representations are each independently projected into a shared space, then fused through a soft, learned gating mechanism \cite{gating_mechanism_citation}:

\begin{equation}
g = \sigma(W[h_{seq} \parallel h_{graph}] + b)
\end{equation}

To prevent one modality from dominating the representation, a balance regularization penalty is applied during training:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{balance} = \lambda \cdot \mathrm{MSE}\!\left(\|h_{seq}\|_{2},\, \|h_{graph}\|_{2}\right)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Training Objective}

All models are optimized under the standard evidence lower bound (ELBO) \cite{elbo_citation}:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \lambda_{recon}\mathcal{L}_{recon} + \beta \, D_{KL}\!\left(q_{\phi}(z|x)\,\|\,p(z)\right)
\end{equation}

KL annealing \cite{kl_annealing_citation} is employed throughout training to suppress posterior collapse. The CNN and GNN models follow a 25-epoch linear warmup for the KL coefficient, while the hybrid model extends this to a 40-epoch schedule to accommodate the additional complexity of fusion learning.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

Latent-space quality is characterized by reconstruction loss, KL divergence, and Active Units (AU) \cite{active_units_citation}. Active Units quantify the fraction of latent dimensions that carry informative signal, defined as:

\begin{equation}
AU = \sum_{j=1}^{d}\mathbb{I}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}(\mu_{j}) > \tau\right)
\end{equation}

where $\tau = 0.01$ and $d = 128$.

\subsection{Targeted Generation Protocol}

Following training, each encoder is evaluated on its practical generation capability through a structured property-targeting procedure. Latent vectors are sampled in regions biased toward nine pre-specified multi-property target configurations (T1--T9), with each configuration prescribing distinct values for target QED, LogP, and SAS. The configurations collectively span a broad pharmacological envelope, from highly drug-like, polar scaffolds to lipophilic and synthetically demanding chemical matter. The complete target matrix is provided in Table~\ref{tab:target_settings}.

