For every encoder--target pairing, 200 molecules are generated. A molecule is classified as a \textit{hit} for a given property when its RDKit-computed value falls within a predefined tolerance band around the target. Three tolerance levels are evaluated:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Strict:} QED $\pm$0.05, LogP $\pm$0.5, SAS $\pm$1.0
    \item \textbf{Medium:} QED $\pm$0.10, LogP $\pm$1.0, SAS $\pm$2.0
    \item \textbf{Lenient:} QED $\pm$0.15, LogP $\pm$1.5, SAS $\pm$3.0
\end{itemize}

The \textit{property hit rate} is defined as the fraction of generated molecules satisfying the tolerance criterion for a single property. The \textit{dual-property hit rate} reports the fraction satisfying QED and LogP constraints simultaneously. In addition, all generated molecules are screened against Lipinski Rule of Five, PAINS, Brenk, and NIH structural alert filters; the fraction passing all four screens is reported as the \textit{safety filter pass rate}. Structural validity (100\%) and novelty against the training corpus (100\%) are verified across all 6,750 molecules produced during the full evaluation.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\subsection{Controlled Variables}

To enable clean attribution of any observed performance difference to encoder architecture, all of the following experimental factors are held invariant across all three model variants:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Latent dimensionality
    \item Decoder architecture and parameterization
    \item Training/test dataset split
    \item Mini-batch size
    \item Training objective and loss coefficients
    \item Reconstruction loss scaling factor
    \item Word dropout rate
    \item Latent injection strategy
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Independent Variable}

The sole experimental variable in this study is the encoder architecture type:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Sequence-based CNN encoder
    \item Graph-based GNN encoder
    \item Hybrid sequence--graph encoder
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Training Configuration}

CNN and GNN models are trained for 25 epochs. The hybrid model undergoes 20 epochs of end-to-end training with the base encoders frozen, combined with an extended KL warmup schedule to stabilize the fusion learning dynamics.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Training Dynamics}

The CNN model exhibits the strongest reconstruction performance across training, converging to a final reconstruction loss of approximately 0.90. This reconstruction advantage comes at a cost: Active Units decline steadily from 128 to roughly 37 over the course of training, indicating that the decoder progressively learns to bypass the majority of latent dimensions---a pattern consistent with latent collapse.

The GNN model displays the opposite tendency, converging to a comparatively weaker reconstruction loss near 1.10 while sustaining approximately 70 Active Units. This higher AU count indicates a substantially more distributed and actively utilized latent representation, even at the expense of reconstruction precision.

The hybrid model occupies an intermediate position, converging to a reconstruction loss near 0.92 with approximately 43 Active Units---a trade-off that reflects the competing dynamics of the two fused encoder branches.

\subsection{Latent Space Analysis}

Variance profiling of the CNN latent space reveals an abundance of near-zero variance dimensions, indicating that information is concentrated into a small subset of latent variables while the remaining dimensions carry little signal.

The GNN latent space exhibits a markedly more diffuse variance distribution with far fewer dormant dimensions. The hybrid encoder partially recovers latent utilization relative to the CNN, but its variance profile remains closer in character to the CNN's concentrated pattern than to the GNN's distributed representation.

\subsection{Reconstruction Behavior}

Token-level reconstruction accuracy across the evaluation set reaches approximately 77\%, while exact sequence-level reconstruction approaches 0\%. This dissociation indicates that the models learn to predict individual tokens with reasonable fidelity but fall short of reconstructing complete molecular sequences with high accuracy---a pattern attributable to the accumulated probability of token-level errors compounding over sequence length.

\subsection{Targeted Generation Performance}

