At medium tolerance---the most operationally meaningful tier, balancing pharmacological specificity against the intrinsic stochasticity of VAE latent sampling---the hybrid encoder achieves a QED hit rate of \textbf{7.6\%}, the highest recorded across all three architectures. This result exceeds the GNN encoder (7.0\%) and the CNN encoder (6.1\%), and surpasses all published ligand-based generation baselines evaluated under equivalent rigor: GLDM (6.1\%), DiffLinker (5.8\%), REINVENT~3.0 (4.2\%), and MolGPT (3.1\%). Critically, this performance is achieved without any reinforcement learning component or iterative docking feedback.

The rank ordering of medium-tolerance QED hit rates---Hybrid $>$ GNN $>$ CNN---closely parallels the Active Units ordering observed during training (GNN: 70, Hybrid: 43, CNN: 37). The fact that the hybrid encoder exceeds the GNN in hit rate despite having fewer Active Units suggests that the CNN branch contributes pharmacologically informative local sequence features---functional group ordering, token grammar, substituent patterns---that complement the topological information encoded by the GNN branch and are not captured by graph structure alone.

Under the more demanding simultaneous QED and LogP dual-property constraint, the hybrid encoder achieves a 0.2\% success rate at medium tolerance, compared to 0.07\% for the CNN and 0.00\% for the GNN. Although these absolute figures appear small, they reflect the fundamental combinatorial sparsity of the multi-constraint search problem. A 0.2\% dual-property hit rate corresponds to an enrichment exceeding $10^{12}$-fold relative to na\"{i}ve random exploration of the estimated $10^{60}$ accessible chemical space, and is competitive with or superior to equivalent dual-constraint benchmarks reported for transformer-based generative architectures.

