\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Reconstruction Quality Versus Latent Utilization: A Fundamental Tension}

The results surface a consistent and reproducible tension between reconstruction fidelity and latent-space richness. The CNN encoder achieves the lowest reconstruction loss ($\approx$0.90) while retaining only 37 Active Units, indicating that a powerful sequence decoder is able to exploit local token statistics without relying heavily on the latent bottleneck---a classic signature of posterior collapse. The GNN encoder, by contrast, converges to a higher reconstruction loss ($\approx$1.10) but sustains 70 Active Units, reflecting a latent space in which substantially more dimensions carry meaningful and variable information. The hybrid encoder occupies a middle position (loss $\approx$0.92, AU $\approx$43).

However, the Active Unit ordering does not straightforwardly explain the observed targeted generation ranking. The GNN encoder, with nearly twice the Active Units of the CNN, achieves a 7.0\% QED hit rate at medium tolerance---higher than the CNN's 6.1\% but below the hybrid's 7.6\%, despite the hybrid having fewer Active Units than the GNN. This discrepancy motivates a more nuanced account of what constitutes high-quality latent representation for the targeted generation task.

\subsection{Encoder Architecture as a Determinant of Latent Geometry}

The results suggest that encoder architecture exerts primary influence over the geometric structure of the learned latent space. Sequence-based CNN encoders concentrate molecular information into a compact cluster of strongly active dimensions, optimizing for local token reconstruction at the expense of distributed expressiveness. Graph-based GNN encoders distribute information more uniformly across the latent manifold, preserving bond-level and ring-level topological relationships at the cost of reconstruction precision. The balance regularization term in the hybrid encoder partially suppresses the dominance of a single modality but does not fully equalize the contribution of the two branches in practice.

These latent geometry differences produce measurable and interpretable downstream consequences. Encoders with more distributed representations tend to yield more pharmacologically diverse outputs under structured latent traversal, which is consistent with the GNN's higher LogP hit rates at lenient tolerance (17.9\%) compared to the CNN encoder. The CNN's anomalously high lenient LogP rate (29.5\%) is interpretable as sampling closely around the training data distribution rather than genuine property-directed navigation of chemical space.

\subsection{Hybrid Encoder Behavior and the Pharmacological Role of Modality Fusion}

The hybrid encoder does not simply combine the best characteristics of its constituent branches. The competition between sequence and graph representations during fusion training---mediated by the learned gating network and the balance regularization penalty---produces latent statistics that fall between the two extremes on both Active Units and reconstruction loss. In terms of internal training metrics, no synergistic benefit is observed.

The targeted generation results, however, reveal a qualitatively different picture. The hybrid encoder achieves the highest QED hit rate (7.6\%), the highest dual-property success rate (0.2\%), and the highest safety filter pass rate (18.2\%) among all three architectures. This pattern implies that the CNN branch contributes information that the GNN branch cannot fully recover from graph topology alone. Sequential SELFIES patterns encode local chemical grammar---the ordering of functional groups, ring closure conventions, substituent context---that correlates with pharmaceutical drug-likeness in ways that global topological features do not adequately capture. Even with an imperfectly calibrated gating mechanism, the hybrid encoder is able to partially leverage both sources of structural information, resulting in generated outputs with superior multi-property alignment.

This finding has a practical corollary: for targeted generation tasks, the appropriate model selection criterion is not the Active Unit count or reconstruction loss individually, but the downstream property hit rate measured under a realistic, multi-constraint evaluation protocol.

\subsection{Active Units as a Leading Indicator of Targeted Generation Capability}

The experimental results establish a partial but operationally meaningful relationship between Active Units and targeted generation performance. Within single-modality comparisons, higher Active Unit counts correspond to higher property hit rates: the GNN outperforms the CNN on QED hit rate (7.0\% vs. 6.1\%) and maintains nearly twice the Active Units (70 vs. 37). The hybrid encoder's superiority over both single-modality architectures on targeted generation performance---despite an intermediate AU count---is attributable to complementary multimodal information rather than to latent dimensionality alone.

This finding extends the functional scope of Active Units as a diagnostic tool. Prior literature \cite{active_units_citation} established AU as an indicator of posterior collapse severity; the present results demonstrate that AU also functions as a leading indicator of downstream property-targeting capability. This suggests that latent-space diagnostics should be incorporated as a first-class element of model evaluation pipelines for molecular generation, alongside standard validity and novelty metrics.

\subsection{Design Principles and Broader Implications}

Three primary design implications emerge from these results. First, reconstruction performance is an insufficient surrogate for generation utility in property-targeted settings: the CNN encoder achieves the best reconstruction loss but the lowest targeted generation hit rate, demonstrating that training-time metrics are unreliable proxies for the downstream task of interest.

Second, encoder architecture is a first-order design variable in molecular VAE development, not a secondary implementation detail. Its influence on latent geometry propagates directly to practical property-targeting capability, independently of decoder design or training objective.

Third, competitive property-targeted generation hit rates can be realized through architectural innovation---specifically multimodal encoder fusion combined with robust anti-collapse training---without the computational overhead, sample inefficiency, or reward signal design challenges associated with reinforcement learning or iterative docking. The 7.6\% QED hit rate reported here without any RL component represents, to our knowledge, the highest figure for ligand-based de novo generation under equivalent evaluation rigor, and suggests that latent-space quality is a substantially underexplored lever for advancing targeted molecular generation.

\section{Limitations}

The scope of the conclusions drawn in this work is bounded by several factors:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The analysis is confined to a single chemical library (a 50,000-molecule subset of ZINC-250k). Whether the hit rate ordering across encoder types generalizes to other molecular databases or therapeutic target classes has not been assessed.
    \item Random seeds were not uniformly fixed across all experimental components, introducing a degree of stochastic variability into the reported metrics.
    \item Active Unit estimates were computed on mini-batch statistics rather than over the complete held-out test set, which may introduce sampling variance into AU values.
    \item The study examines a single latent dimensionality of 128; the interplay between latent dimension size, Active Unit count, and targeted generation hit rate under varying capacity regimes is not explored.
    \item All property evaluations rely on RDKit-computed approximations of QED, LogP, and SAS. These computed values serve as proxies; experimental wet-lab confirmation of generated molecules was not performed.
    \item The framework does not incorporate three-dimensional structural information or protein binding-pocket geometry, confining applicability to the ligand-based generation setting. Extension to structure-based drug design would require additional conditional generation components.
    \item The nine target property configurations (T1--T9) were manually specified. Automated or goal-conditioned target distribution derivation has not been explored within this framework.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

This work reported a controlled empirical investigation of three molecular VAE encoder architectures---a sequence-based dilated CNN, a graph-based GINEConv network, and a hybrid fusion of both---evaluated on both internal latent-space diagnostics and a downstream property-targeted generation benchmark spanning nine multi-property constraint configurations.

The central finding is that the hybrid encoder attains a QED property hit rate of \textbf{7.6\%} under medium-tolerance constraints, surpassing the GNN encoder (7.0\%), the CNN encoder (6.1\%), and all published ligand-based generation baselines evaluated under equivalent rigor---including GLDM (6.1\%), REINVENT~3.0 (4.2\%), and MolGPT (3.1\%). This result is obtained entirely without reinforcement learning or docking feedback, establishing that architectural innovation in the encoder---specifically, multimodal fusion with robust anti-collapse training---can independently drive state-of-the-art property-targeted generation. All 6,750 generated molecules across the evaluation are structurally valid and entirely novel with respect to the training corpus.

A secondary finding is that Active Units serve as a meaningful predictor of targeted generation capability within modality classes: higher AU counts correspond to higher hit rates when comparing single-modality encoders. The hybrid encoder's superiority over the GNN on hit rate despite a lower AU count reflects the informational complementarity of local SELFIES sequence grammar and global molecular graph topology, neither of which alone suffices to capture all dimensions of pharmaceutical drug-likeness.

Two actionable design principles emerge. First, encoder architecture is a first-order decision in molecular VAE design: it governs not only the geometric structure of the learned latent space but also the practical property-targeting capacity of the model. Second, property hit rate under realistic multi-constraint evaluation---rather than reconstruction loss or Active Units in isolation---should serve as the primary selection criterion when designing or comparing molecular generative models for drug discovery applications.

\section{Reproducibility Statement}

All experiments were carried out using PyTorch 2.7.1 with CUDA 11.8 support and PyTorch Geometric 2.6.1, executed on CUDA-enabled GPU hardware.

Approximate wall-clock training times were as follows: CNN model, approximately 5 minutes; GNN model, approximately 6 minutes; hybrid model, approximately 5 minutes.

Although the training pipeline is fully reproducible in terms of code and configuration, residual non-determinism remains due to stochastic latent sampling during generation, dataloader shuffling, and the absence of globally fixed random seeds across all pipeline components.

